301: \begin{enumerate}
302:   \item \textbf{Replica Availability:} Inspects the \texttt{coredns} Deployment for \texttt{availableReplicas} vs. \texttt{expectedReplicas} (default: 2).
303:   \item \textbf{Pod Readiness:} Identifies pods in \texttt{CrashLoopBackOff}, \texttt{Error}, or unready states.
304:   \item \textbf{DNS Query Latency:} Queries Prometheus for average request duration over a 1-minute window:
305:   \begin{equation}
306:     \text{Latency} = \frac{\texttt{rate(sum[1m])}}{\texttt{rate(count[1m])}}
307:     \label{eq:latency}
308:   \end{equation}
309:   \item \textbf{Upstream Configuration:} Parses the Corefile ConfigMap to validate \texttt{forward} directive targets against known-bad subnets (RFC~5737~\cite{rfc5737}: \texttt{192.0.2.0/24}, \texttt{198.51.100.0/24}, \texttt{203.0.113.0/24}).
310:   \item \textbf{Resource Starvation:} Detects CPU limits $\leq$5\,m that cause latency spikes despite pods appearing healthy.
311: \end{enumerate}
312: 
313: \subsubsection{Remediation Actions}
314: \begin{itemize}
315:   \item \textbf{Scale Recovery:} Patches the CoreDNS Deployment replicas back to \texttt{expectedReplicas}.
316:   \item \textbf{Pod Restart:} Deletes unready/crashlooping pods to trigger clean respawns.
317:   \item \textbf{ConfigMap Repair:} Replaces corrupted \texttt{forward} targets with fallback servers (default: \texttt{/etc/resolv.conf}, \texttt{8.8.8.8}) and triggers a rolling restart.
318:   \item \textbf{Resource Restoration:} Resets CPU limits to baseline (100\,m) when throttling is detected.
319: \end{itemize}
320: 
321: Each remediation type is gated by independent safety toggles (\texttt{autoHealReplicas}, \texttt{autoHealConfigMap}) and a 30\,s cooldown window to prevent restart thrashing.
322: 
323: \subsection{NetworkPolicy Module}
324: 
325: \subsubsection{Detection Signals}
326: The NetworkPolicy module targets the \emph{control-plane-to-dataplane disconnect} described in Section~III-C. It audits all \texttt{calico-node} pods in \texttt{kube-system} for:
327: \begin{itemize}
328:   \item Pod phase $\neq$ \texttt{Running}
329:   \item Container readiness $=$ \texttt{false}
330:   \item Container restart count $\geq$~3
331:   \item Container state: \texttt{CrashLoopBackOff} or \texttt{Error}
332: \end{itemize}
333: 
334: Any pod matching these criteria indicates that the Felix enforcement agent is not actively programming kernel packet-filtering rules, leaving the dataplane in a stale state.
335: 
336: \subsubsection{Remediation Actions}
337: When unhealthy enforcement agents are detected and \texttt{autoHeal} is enabled, the operator deletes the degraded \texttt{calico-node} pod. The DaemonSet controller respawns a fresh pod, and on startup, Felix reads all declared policies from the Kubernetes API and programs \texttt{iptables}/\texttt{ipset} chains from scratch, clearing stale rules. A configurable cooldown (default: 60\,s) rate-limits consecutive restarts to prevent control plane churn.
338: 
339: \subsection{Pod-to-Pod Connectivity Module}
340: 
341: \subsubsection{Probing Topology}
342: We adapt the Pingmesh~\cite{pingmesh2015} approach to Kubernetes by establishing a deterministic ring topology across $N$ worker nodes. Nodes are sorted lexicographically and connected as:
343: \begin{equation}
344:   \text{Forward: } Node_i \to Node_{(i+1) \bmod N}
345: \end{equation}
346: \begin{equation}
347:   \text{Reverse: } Node_i \to Node_{(i-1+N) \bmod N} \quad (N \geq 3)
348: \end{equation}
349: 
350: This yields exactly $2N$ inter-node probes, achieving $O(N)$ scalability versus the $O(N^2)$ cost of full-mesh probing. Each node additionally undergoes an $O(1)$ intra-node canary validation to verify local veth, bridge, and iptables FORWARD chain integrity.
351: 
352: \subsubsection{Three-Tier Triangulation}
353: The evaluate phase employs a three-tier pattern analysis engine to isolate root causes and prevent false remediation:
354: 
355: \noindent\textbf{Tier~1 - Local CNI Validation:} If a node's local probe fails but its system anchors (gateway, CoreDNS) are reachable, the root cause is isolated to the local CNI subsystem (dropped veth, corrupted eBPF map).
356: 
357: \noindent\textbf{Tier~2 - Inter-Node Triangulation:} When $Node_A \not\to Node_B$, the operator checks if $Node_C \to Node_B$ succeeds. If all peers fail to reach $Node_B$, the ingress network stack on $Node_B$ is declared dead. If only $Node_A$ fails, the issue is an asymmetric path/tunnel drop.
358: 
359: \noindent\textbf{Tier~3 - Anchor Corroboration:} For small clusters ($N < 3$) where triangulation is insufficient, probes against high-availability anchors (default gateway, \texttt{kube-apiserver}) disambiguate host-level versus tunnel-level failures.
360: 
361: Fig.~\ref{fig:triangulation} illustrates the three-tier triangulation decision tree.
362: 
